# JMarkdown `@` directives

Inline, both groups: @callout[Watch out — this is *important* and has math $e^{i\pi}=-1$.]{.warning #first data-level="3"}

Inline, angle form: @<fig>[See /figure one/ below.]{.centered}

Block directive (note the `+`): @aside+[A block-level aside with a ==highlighted== phrase and a `code span`.]{.sidebar style="color: crimson; font-weight: bold"}

Block, angle + plus: @<callout>+[Another block, this time with an @callout[nested inline]{.inner} directive.]{#outer}

## Optional groups

Bare name, no groups: @linebreak here.

Text only, no attributes: @ref[section-intro].

Attributes only, no text: @hr{.thin .dashed}.

## Line breaks inside the groups

@summary[
  The text group may run across
  several lines, as long as no blank
  line appears inside it.
]{.boxed data-collapsible="true"}

## Not directives

An email like jason@example.com is left alone, and an unpaired @<angle stays plain.

@begin(theorem)[Euclid]{.numbered}
There are infinitely many primes. (This is an environment, not an `@`-directive.)
@end(theorem)
